WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
- I really hope that people
also, if they read it,

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.360
realize this is not a book
that is meant to over coddle

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.910
the millennials or gen Z,

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.350
this is truly believing

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:17.762
that this is the alpha infrastructure

00:00:17.762 --> 00:00:22.051
to building significantly
large successful businesses.

00:00:22.051 --> 00:00:22.884
Hey!

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.382
You got your perspective.

00:00:26.382 --> 00:00:29.382
(audience cheering)

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.217
I just wanna be happy,
don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:32.217 --> 00:00:35.169
(upbeat music)

00:00:35.169 --> 00:00:39.900
It is very clear to me,
25 years into my career

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.900
that kindness and empathy
and patience and humility

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.620
are actually the foundations.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:51.380
And I think the things
that all of us think about

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:55.331
as soft skills and emotional intelligence

00:00:55.331 --> 00:00:59.880
are actually the hard
skills that it is far easier

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.730
for me to find people
who are good at the task

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:08.620
or have good math skills
than it is to find people

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:12.390
who are capable of being the
bigger woman or bigger man

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:15.230
in the face of pressure or adversity.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:20.230
And so for 20 years or so, this
has been in my subconscious.

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:25.240
COVID, like for many of us
gave me a time to reflect,

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.161
and I immediately went
into writing the book

00:01:28.161 --> 00:01:30.610
on a couple of reasons.

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:34.550
One I'm incredibly aware that
there's a lot of young people

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:38.470
paying attention to my moves,
are fans, of what I do.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:41.860
And I thought it would be a disservice

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:46.860
if I wasn't incredibly
aggressive in my communication

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.259
on the things that I see
as a winning formula,

00:01:51.259 --> 00:01:55.920
it becomes double important
when I realize that most people

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.820
don't talk about kindness
as the single alpha skill

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.460
in building a successful empire.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.370
And I thought it was a unique take.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:05.870
It's also very nuanced.

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:08.380
The reason the subtitle is ingredients

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:10.010
is what I was going through, my process.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:13.480
I realized, oh, I never
just deploy empathy.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.350
I never just deploy patience.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:16.726
In every situation

00:02:16.726 --> 00:02:19.200
it's actually three or
four different traits,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.980
often traits that are in a
conflict with each other.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.460
It's very interesting to
be tenacious and ambitious

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.300
while being patient.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:32.540
And so I liked the concept of ingredients

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.330
because it's like cooking a meal.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.837
I think we all believe
that sweet and sour chicken

00:02:36.837 --> 00:02:38.970
is something that is delicious,

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.140
but sweet and sour can see a conflict.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:45.200
So I felt that I was the right
person at the right time.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.390
And what has transpired
in the last 18 months,

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.490
'cause I wrote the book very quickly

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:53.340
in the beginning of COVID,
obviously takes time

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.200
for it to come out and
things of that nature,

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.410
many of us are thinking about
things in the business world,

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:02.698
around the concept of
the great resignation

00:03:02.698 --> 00:03:05.830
and the concept of people even applying

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.940
into businesses in the first
place, in a world of options.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:13.290
I think the importance of being a leader

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:17.190
that understands these
traits has even compounded

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:20.040
in the last 18 months
since I even wrote it,

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.523
which feels serendipitous
and exciting for me.

00:03:23.523 --> 00:03:28.020
And I really hope that
people also, if they read it,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:32.810
realize this is not a book
that is meant to over coddle

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.360
the millennials or gen Z,

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:38.020
this is truly believing that

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:41.600
this is the alpha infrastructure

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.985
to building significantly
large successful businesses.

00:03:45.985 --> 00:03:49.940
- What I love is this
refreshing take on leadership.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.180
And I can speak from a woman's perspective

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.050
that many of us have mimicked

00:03:56.965 --> 00:04:01.350
this ideal hard leadership
because that was the architect.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.267
That was what was the right leadership

00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:08.160
and you're taking the narrative
into a different direction.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.910
And so what would you say to those

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.430
who would point to titans
of industries and say,

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:16.820
well, this is not, how they were,

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:20.290
this is not what you're
describing the world.

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:23.570
- Look, I believe that you
can have short term success

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.720
in many ways and I believe that the world

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:29.200
has been very clear to us
over the last centuries

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.540
that fear and negativity
are sometimes a fuel

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:34.403
for short term success.

00:04:35.307 --> 00:04:40.307
My argument is that it's not as powerful

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:45.660
as brightness and love, that
hate is a powerful trait,

00:04:46.754 --> 00:04:51.590
but I just believe it never
beats love in the end.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:53.460
And I believe that to be true in business,

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.630
of course there have been
people who've been successful

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:58.990
who are not kind leaders.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:02.400
That doesn't mean that
it should be mimicked.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.480
And I would also ask
if you have the luxury

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.440
of interacting with people in
their eighties and nineties

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:15.230
and especially those who've
had major financial success

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:18.800
by being mean and negative.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.500
I think you'll find that
many 80, 90 year olds

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.230
who built their empire with darkness

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:26.350
have incredible levels of regret

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:29.040
and actually live quite unhappy lives.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.040
And so my question simply
to business people is,

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:35.573
is the money worth the unhappiness.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:40.702
- You talk a lot about
empathy and kindness.

00:05:40.702 --> 00:05:41.720
And I think how many times

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:44.520
have we gone through
the last couple of years

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.630
with everything that's
happening in the world,

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:49.124
we need more kindness
and empathy than ever.

00:05:49.124 --> 00:05:54.077
And the question here is how
do you deploy these attributes

00:05:54.077 --> 00:05:57.990
without enabling that behavior, without?

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:01.990
- Well, I think first of all,
people want everybody else

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:03.603
to be kind and empathetic.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.233
So my favorite part of this
book is accountability.

00:06:09.233 --> 00:06:14.120
Everyone has become incredibly
good at judging others.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.540
The world is addicted to pointing fingers.

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:20.820
I have never seen the world louder

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:23.743
about telling everybody
else what they're bad at.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.620
So I think the way to deploy
successful kindness and empathy

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:32.020
is to actually start with yourself.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:35.900
I also wanna remind everybody
that deploying kindness

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:39.960
when the other person
kind to you is very easy.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.700
I challenge the world to
start having a conversation

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:45.600
with itself of does it have the ability

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.713
to be kind and empathetic
in the face of negativity?

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.760
My leaders are incredibly
kind to my employees

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.640
when everything's going well,

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.020
I don't need peace time generals.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:02.810
I need leaders when
things are bad and hectic

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:05.370
to be able to be the
bigger woman and bigger man

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:08.110
in that situation to create a framework

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:10.270
of kindness and empathy.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:14.840
So, you know, for me, it's
about the accountability

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.650
of being able to be that person.

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:18.460
Right now we are in a world

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:23.010
where everyone is so quick to
get triggered by bad behavior

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:26.580
that they fight fire with
fire which is why so little

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.340
is being accomplished
in society, in politics

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:32.020
and in the workforce.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:36.270
And so for me, I think that we
are not spending enough time

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:39.270
with our thumbs and we
need to stop being addicted

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:40.423
to our fingers.

00:07:41.539 --> 00:07:42.372
- Great.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:46.001
One of the things you speak
about is self-awareness,

00:07:46.001 --> 00:07:49.904
which is foundational
to any evolution for us

00:07:49.904 --> 00:07:50.793
as human beings.

00:07:50.793 --> 00:07:53.460
Tell us a bit about that and how do you,

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:55.416
where do we start with self-awareness?

00:07:55.416 --> 00:07:57.960
- This is hard.

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.720
The concept of teaching
someone to be more self aware

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.510
is something I've been
thinking about for a long time.

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:04.343
You know, I think the thing

00:08:04.343 --> 00:08:07.470
that drives so many of
us crazy in the world

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:11.620
is interacting with those that
struggle with self-awareness.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:14.950
I think one of the hardest
things for people to deal with

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:16.560
is hypocrisy.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:19.025
We struggle with it
and I struggle with it.

00:08:19.025 --> 00:08:23.560
The exercise in the book
that I decided to do

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:26.830
was creating frameworks

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:30.980
for the people that know you
the best to create a safe space

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:33.660
for them to be able to tell you the truth.

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:36.960
I think one of the reasons people struggle

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.069
with self-awareness is
because they have enablers

00:08:40.069 --> 00:08:42.410
around them that are allowing them

00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:44.820
to get away with bad behavior.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:48.310
And so I'm spending a lot of
time trying to think about

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:52.020
how one puts the people
that knows them the best

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:55.810
in a safe place to actually
give them the critical feedback

00:08:55.810 --> 00:09:00.030
that could be the jar of a
journey towards self awareness.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:01.460
It's a very tough challenge.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:03.750
I don't know if I have the answer.

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:08.750
What I can tell you is no
question the business leaders

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:12.150
that I admire, the human
beings that I admire

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:16.030
are the ones who are off the
charts in self-awareness,

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:20.230
I'm incredibly grateful for
the luck of the DNA game

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.090
that I think gave me a lot of it.

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:25.640
I would argue that the only
reason I have any level

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:29.020
of popularity is because of self-awareness

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:33.150
because of the kind of communicator
I am and my personality,

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:36.380
I would be completely not palpable

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:39.440
if I didn't have high
levels of self-awareness

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:41.560
'cause I have some really funny attributes

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:42.900
as a communicator.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.176
And so it's a conversation
we need more of.

00:09:46.176 --> 00:09:49.350
I do think that self-awareness

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:52.926
has a very interesting
relationship with accountability.

00:09:52.926 --> 00:09:56.170
I think that a lot of people actually know

00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:59.110
what their shortcomings are,

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:03.540
but they try to navigate
the world in a way

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:07.710
where they are hoping
that people don't notice.

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:10.393
And it's something I'm
thinking a lot about.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.290
- One of the things I
actually loved in the book

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:17.290
is the part where you talk
about relationship with time.

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:18.123
- Yes.

00:10:18.123 --> 00:10:20.620
- And you hear often people
say it's too late for me

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:23.866
to start a new business, to
move my career, to be married,

00:10:23.866 --> 00:10:25.223
whatever that may be.

00:10:25.223 --> 00:10:28.487
So I'd love for you to
give us that perspective.

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:32.830
- The rules of the world right now

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:36.210
on when you should have
your life figured out,

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:41.210
when you should be married,
have children, make money,

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:46.970
buy a home and everything
else are based completely

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:51.700
on when people live to 45
and 55 years old, period,

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:54.018
the rules of our society have not changed

00:10:54.018 --> 00:10:58.030
in a very long time of expectation

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:00.610
on what we should be doing with our lives.

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:03.813
The problem with that
is we're living to 100.

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:07.680
The concept that a 25 year old

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:12.430
should have everything
figured out is laughable.

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:15.799
Most 60 year olds I know don't
have their life figured out.

00:11:15.799 --> 00:11:19.410
Why are 25 year olds put in such pressure?

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:23.040
I think that we have
societally done a very poor job

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:24.450
to our youth,

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.480
which gets me to a place that's
even more emotional for me,

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:32.480
which is we must challenge the notion

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:38.030
that so many parents in the
world use their kids' success

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:40.513
as their own self-esteem.

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:43.790
What that does is it pressures children

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:48.790
into short term behaviors
around education, occupation,

00:11:49.027 --> 00:11:53.120
and relationships that are often missteps

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:57.595
based on very small windows of time.

00:11:57.595 --> 00:12:00.190
One of the great reasons I struggle

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:02.956
with the way that education
is sold in our society

00:12:02.956 --> 00:12:06.320
is we train kids from a very young age

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:10.771
that you will be judged every
90 days on your actions.

00:12:10.771 --> 00:12:15.100
I think it leads to very,
very vulnerable behavior

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:17.280
that maps to our adulthood.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:21.370
So yes, I think that way too many people

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.970
are judging themselves in the short term.

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:27.470
Yes I believe that if
you are 63 years old,

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:28.910
you still have 30 years

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:30.823
to achieve fulfillment and happiness.

00:12:30.823 --> 00:12:32.250
And I think it's time

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:35.690
that we have a total different
conversation around age

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:38.038
to the point where in actuality,

00:12:38.038 --> 00:12:40.300
the fact that we pressure children

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:45.130
into starting to get serious
at 22 is actually ludicrous.

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:49.980
22 to 30 should be wild, wild, high risk,

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:52.960
high reward behavior
because it's practical

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:55.948
because even if you blow
it and nothing goes well,

00:12:55.948 --> 00:12:59.430
you can start getting serious at 30, 35.

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:03.360
And so we ask people to now
you're a grownup get a real job.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:06.620
And all this other ludicrous behavior

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:08.530
that I think has really hurt society.

00:13:08.530 --> 00:13:13.530
I think a lot of it is predicated
on outside affirmation.

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:17.780
I think many people start
doing things for validation

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:21.495
from strangers or their parents or others.

00:13:21.495 --> 00:13:23.743
And I think we have to have
more thoughtful conversations

00:13:23.743 --> 00:13:25.030
about it.

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:30.030
I think it's quite depressing
for lack of a better word,

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:33.140
that there are people
that are 40 years old,

00:13:33.140 --> 00:13:38.140
still have 60 years ahead of
them and they think it's over.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.273
I think that needs to stop.

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:44.300
- So one of the things that
surprised me in the book

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:45.575
is your health.

00:13:45.575 --> 00:13:49.319
- Mm that's because Gary V
the guy that sits up here

00:13:49.319 --> 00:13:51.180
is the king of candor.

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:52.640
- Exactly.
- I shoot it

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:56.320
straight as an arrow when
I'm talking to the world.

00:13:56.320 --> 00:13:59.520
But when Gary Vaynerchuk the
executive is sitting across

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:02.330
from an employee that
he loves and he's scared

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:05.120
to tell her or him that
they're bad at their job

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:08.040
because he fears that that's
gonna put them into a spiral

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:10.716
of being scared that
they're about to be fired

00:14:10.716 --> 00:14:14.315
is a lesson that has taken
me over 20 years to learn.

00:14:14.315 --> 00:14:17.980
When I start started
really reflecting on why

00:14:18.890 --> 00:14:22.733
in a world where I really
don't care about the money,

00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:26.810
why would I have any employee
that has ever worked with me,

00:14:26.810 --> 00:14:28.480
be unhappy with me?

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.720
And I had to look in
the mirror and realize

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:32.900
that the greatest shortcoming of my life

00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:37.100
let alone my professional
career has been my inability

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:40.290
to be candorous about my
opinions and feelings,

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:43.990
which has led to subconscious
and then conscious resentment

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:47.340
towards that individual,
which has led to conflict,

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:50.660
which has led to me
randomly firing somebody

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:52.380
out of left field for them,

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:56.270
even though I thought they
should have known all along.

00:14:56.270 --> 00:15:00.890
That was a very difficult thing
for me to come to grips with

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:04.090
because my greatest pride as a leader

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:06.140
is the elimination of fear.

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:09.180
When I realized that my lack of candor

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.320
was actually creating macro fear

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:14.060
because nobody actually knew
where they stood with me.

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:16.810
It was one of the darkest
days of my career,

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:19.300
but it was also one of the
greatest days of my career,

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:21.240
'cause it started the process

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.180
of me starting to figure it out

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:26.510
and me being vulnerable
and putting it in my book

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:28.280
for everyone to see,

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:31.910
especially when my reputation
as a content creator

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:35.340
is that is my strength
was an important step

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:36.993
in my own evolution.

00:15:36.993 --> 00:15:41.993
- And what helped you actually
evolve that space like that?

00:15:42.122 --> 00:15:45.500
- Reading Facebook groups
of former employees

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:47.863
that worked for me saying I was a jerk.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:51.300
It's incredibly challenging for me.

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:55.000
I genuinely don't like money enough

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:56.590
to have people not like me.

00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:57.920
I don't know what else to tell you.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:00.629
It's a complete brain twist
for me that this could be.

00:16:00.629 --> 00:16:04.710
And I finally had to
take on accountability

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:08.610
because it was very easy for
me to blame poor performers

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:12.220
because everybody around them
also thought they sucked.

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:15.200
So it was easy for me to justify,
like they were tone deaf.

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:16.870
They weren't self aware,

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:19.830
but the reality is I never
gave them a chance to fix it

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:20.720
because I wasn't willing

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:22.765
to give them constructive criticism.

00:16:22.765 --> 00:16:27.765
And I, really so much of my strength

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:31.134
and so much of this book
is really just a story

00:16:31.134 --> 00:16:34.213
of a boy who idolizes his mom,

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:37.970
but his mom also isn't great at candor.

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:39.850
And he picked up on that as well,

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:43.680
who also had a father who
was exceptional at candor.

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:47.530
But his delivery of that
candor was so terrible

00:16:47.530 --> 00:16:49.780
that he demonized the candor

00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:51.960
without realizing it was the vehicle

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:54.960
that the candor was being
delivered in that was the problem,

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:55.904
not the candor.

00:16:55.904 --> 00:16:59.680
And so that was a process of
self-reflection and experience.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:01.610
And for all the kids in here,

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:03.330
no matter how great you think you are.

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:05.528
And I thought I was super great at 22,

00:17:05.528 --> 00:17:07.190
let me give you a preview.

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:09.883
The gray hairs come with some value.

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:16.096
- So on another attribute, optimism.

00:17:16.096 --> 00:17:16.929
- Mm.

00:17:16.929 --> 00:17:20.710
- So it's easy to be optimistic
when things are good,

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:22.364
when you face a hurdle or two,

00:17:22.364 --> 00:17:25.190
but if your life is all you ever did,

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:27.061
if it's everything you ever learnt,

00:17:27.061 --> 00:17:29.728
where do you draw optimism from?

00:17:30.716 --> 00:17:34.199
- Well, this is where the
ingredients come into play.

00:17:34.199 --> 00:17:37.543
My optimism comes from gratitude.

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:42.170
When you are grateful for what you have

00:17:42.170 --> 00:17:45.150
versus envious for what you don't have.

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:47.323
Optimism comes second nature.

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:52.090
Life is always going to be challenging,

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:56.270
but perspective is something
we all need a lot more of.

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:59.513
We sit here today in this gorgeous room

00:17:59.513 --> 00:18:04.513
while 850 million, almost 10% of the world

00:18:04.773 --> 00:18:09.773
does not have access to clean water.

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:16.030
850 million people in this world

00:18:16.030 --> 00:18:20.310
do not have access to clean water.

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:23.820
I have not been able to
put the pieces together

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:26.716
to be upset about a
business deal going awry

00:18:26.716 --> 00:18:30.680
in a world where 850 million other people

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:33.010
don't have access to clean water.

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:36.770
I am dumbfounded by people's inability

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:38.700
to be grateful for what they have.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:42.880
For me optimism is second nature
'cause I'm completely based

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:47.880
in fundamental gratitude and
I wish more people were too.

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.560
And that's why I like
talking about gratitude.

00:18:52.527 --> 00:18:57.457
- Those attributes that
you're speaking about,

00:18:57.457 --> 00:19:00.457
they're beautifully selected

00:19:00.457 --> 00:19:02.910
and the way that we put
those ingredients together

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:06.960
actually are so practical in
terms of how broad is this book

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:10.168
to evolve and to kind of reflect.

00:19:10.168 --> 00:19:12.355
- Can I jump in on that?

00:19:12.355 --> 00:19:13.570
- Yes.

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:16.470
- It's incredibly important
especially for those in here

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.690
who don't have a lot of context on me.

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:21.606
I've been listening to the
first 10, 15 minutes of this.

00:19:21.606 --> 00:19:25.820
Let there be no confusion I
am a full pledged operator.

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:28.440
I am only grounded in practicality.

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:31.860
For 22 of the last years
I have been responsible

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:34.659
to make payroll 100% on my own back.

00:19:34.659 --> 00:19:38.990
Everything I'm talking about
right now is the framework

00:19:38.990 --> 00:19:40.260
for those successes.

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:42.840
Yet I could be very empathetic

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:45.070
that somebody listening
right now may think

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:49.823
that this is a very optimistic,
delusional ideology.

00:19:51.310 --> 00:19:54.070
That is why I wrote the book.

00:19:54.070 --> 00:19:56.973
This is the furthest thing from delusion,

00:19:57.810 --> 00:20:01.930
but society for over 100 years of business

00:20:01.930 --> 00:20:04.670
have told all of you that
the things I'm talking about

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:09.670
almost have no place in business
or is a very nice to have

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:11.870
as long as you have all the other skills,

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:15.948
I promise you all those
other skills are a commodity.

00:20:15.948 --> 00:20:19.650
The reason most businesses fail
is 'cause they're incapable

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:23.510
of firing the people that
are producing dollars,

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:24.823
but are destroying morale.

00:20:26.134 --> 00:20:29.384
(audience applauding)

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:36.546
- Maybe one more question.

00:20:36.546 --> 00:20:38.823
Before we take questions
from the audience.

00:20:38.823 --> 00:20:41.156
Gary, with these attributes,

00:20:41.994 --> 00:20:44.700
is this something that
actually we can work on

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:49.700
or are we capped at, whether
we're born with empathy or not?

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:52.070
- This is a great question.

00:20:52.070 --> 00:20:55.743
I believe that we are born
with a lot of our traits.

00:20:55.743 --> 00:20:58.030
I believe we are then parented.

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:00.040
And some of the traits go up and down.

00:21:00.040 --> 00:21:02.790
I believe we are environments
of our neighborhood,

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:04.703
our country, our culture.

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:09.680
When I tell you the thing
that comes least natural

00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:13.220
to me in the world is candor one on one.

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:17.490
And for me to see where I am
today as a 46 year old man,

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:20.120
everybody in this room has the ability

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:23.380
to grow in many of these attributes.

00:21:23.380 --> 00:21:25.713
Most don't want to put in the work.

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:28.813
Most would prefer not to do the things

00:21:28.813 --> 00:21:30.655
that come hard to them.

00:21:30.655 --> 00:21:35.360
Most would hope that it goes unnoticed.

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:37.590
Nothing goes unnoticed.

00:21:37.590 --> 00:21:41.340
People may not tell you,
but nothing goes unnoticed.

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:43.740
I believe in the human spirit,

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:46.960
the world is very black and
white to me on this one.

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:50.210
People evolve, people change,

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:53.090
people can get stronger at certain things.

00:21:53.090 --> 00:21:55.050
People can get weaker at certain things.

00:21:55.050 --> 00:22:00.050
And so I am incredibly
optimistic on humans' capacity

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:03.320
to evolve.

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:07.683
What it requires is first
acknowledging its truth.

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:12.987
Seeing it, one of the
reasons I produce the content

00:22:12.987 --> 00:22:16.730
that I produce is with
the hopes that just,

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:20.590
when I do this right now,
literally when I tell you

00:22:20.590 --> 00:22:23.480
that I'm just hopeful that
one person in this room

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:28.964
is listening and something triggers, one,

00:22:28.964 --> 00:22:31.080
because that's profound.

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:34.250
Saying a couple of sentences

00:22:34.250 --> 00:22:37.790
that shift a person's perspective

00:22:37.790 --> 00:22:40.970
which allows them to start building

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:43.177
towards a better place for themselves

00:22:43.177 --> 00:22:46.130
and the people they love is a great gift.

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:47.990
And I take on that responsibility.

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:51.040
And I think everybody in this room

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:55.250
who is fortunate enough
to be in a happy place,

00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:59.050
has a requirement to
start communicating it

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:02.720
because we live in a world
today where negativity

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:07.720
is incredibly loud and
positivity tends to be silent.

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:11.810
And I think if you are positive,

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:14.355
you have an obligation to your fellow man

00:23:14.355 --> 00:23:17.003
to communicate it and make it louder.

00:23:18.022 --> 00:23:21.272
(audience applauding)

00:23:26.233 --> 00:23:28.510
- Perhaps we take a few
questions from the audience?

00:23:28.510 --> 00:23:31.020
- Yeah, these microphones
that are lined up here

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:32.443
excite me to no end.

00:23:32.443 --> 00:23:35.880
I hope that tons of people,
I hope tons of people

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:36.780
or is that how we're doing it?

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:38.055
People are gonna come up to mics, please?

00:23:38.055 --> 00:23:41.560
I mean, I see a lot of hands,
line up, there's a lot of mics

00:23:41.560 --> 00:23:42.760
let's line up and do it.

00:23:43.699 --> 00:23:44.929
Thank you.

00:23:44.929 --> 00:23:46.880
- [Audience Member 1] Okay I used to be,

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:49.454
signed this couple of years
when I so vividly remember

00:23:49.454 --> 00:23:52.267
that one day I was honest

00:23:52.267 --> 00:23:53.920
about my company being compassionate,

00:23:53.920 --> 00:23:55.480
where they came up to me and they're like,

00:23:55.480 --> 00:23:56.780
you're supposed to do your job

00:23:56.780 --> 00:23:58.367
when I was looking after someone,

00:23:58.367 --> 00:24:02.750
that was the day I decided
that I didn't want to do this.

00:24:02.750 --> 00:24:05.589
And I was looking at your
content all these days

00:24:05.589 --> 00:24:07.040
and that was my trigger point.

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:09.430
And I decided to pursue
photography on the weekends

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:10.550
and now it's been four years

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:13.929
I've done that full time
freelance, I freaking love it.

00:24:13.929 --> 00:24:14.762
And (indistinct) career in arts saved

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:20.900
and that was my savior.

00:24:20.900 --> 00:24:22.993
And now I'm into NFTs

00:24:22.993 --> 00:24:25.810
and I'm trying to bring
more people into this space.

00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:28.410
I'm building a company in the NFT space.

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:31.830
What I'm trying to do is I
want get most of my friends

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:33.164
into this.

00:24:33.164 --> 00:24:35.750
I feel like the NFT
economies, creator economies

00:24:35.750 --> 00:24:36.700
is changing the world.

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:39.280
But I struggle with it and
I wanna know about this,

00:24:39.280 --> 00:24:41.315
'cause you talk about
empathy and compassion.

00:24:41.315 --> 00:24:44.560
And I regard myself as a
very compassionate person

00:24:44.560 --> 00:24:48.920
so much so that sometimes
I want to help my friends

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:50.340
to get into the space.

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:53.284
And I'm unable to do that
'cause of their liabilities

00:24:53.284 --> 00:24:54.330
and their responsibilities.

00:24:54.330 --> 00:24:57.130
How do you, 'cause I know you
did say about general people

00:24:57.130 --> 00:25:00.400
that you have the obligation
to explain to them,

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:01.920
but with your friends and families,

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:05.960
how do you come to terms
that you can't do so much

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:06.969
for everyone?

00:25:06.969 --> 00:25:11.257
- Because I deploy patience,

00:25:11.257 --> 00:25:14.072
everyone's on a different time schedule.

00:25:14.072 --> 00:25:16.930
One of the great things that works for me

00:25:16.930 --> 00:25:19.023
is a lack of expectations.

00:25:20.420 --> 00:25:24.910
You know, I want everybody
that I ever talk to

00:25:24.910 --> 00:25:26.651
to see what I see.

00:25:26.651 --> 00:25:28.890
I'm also incredibly aware

00:25:28.890 --> 00:25:33.143
that that is not going to
be the case on my timeline.

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:37.270
You've gotta realize that
even though you're coming

00:25:37.270 --> 00:25:38.660
from a beautiful place,

00:25:38.660 --> 00:25:41.883
the energy that you're talking
about right now is selfish.

00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:45.930
You wanna feel the endorphin hit

00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:47.315
of bringing value to somebody you love,

00:25:47.315 --> 00:25:51.690
which is a wild concept to
realize that it's selfish,

00:25:51.690 --> 00:25:56.163
all you can do is stay consistent
with your communication.

00:25:57.670 --> 00:26:00.995
I mean, there are certain
things that I taught my father

00:26:00.995 --> 00:26:03.993
that took me 26 years to accomplish.

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:11.199
If you love them, you
won't stop communicating.

00:26:11.199 --> 00:26:13.157
- [Audience Member 1]
Awesome, thank you so much.

00:26:13.157 --> 00:26:16.955
(audience applauding)

